1.1 | Funding challenges for collaborative biostatistics units
When 100% of one's time is allocated to specific research projects or groups, several challenges arise for the collaborative biostatistician. First,
the collaborative biostatistician does not have time allotted to tasks that they are required to complete but are not necessarily linked to a specific
research project or group. This includes administrative tasks such as attending meetings for their own unit, department, or college; tracking their
working time; completing performance evaluations; and institutional or professional service (if required). It also includes time that the collabora
tive biostatistician spends on their own professional development, which is an important part of being a professional but is often neglected due to
the lack of time available for it under this funding model (Taylor et al., 2023; Welty et al., 2013). Some collaborative biostatisticians, especially
those at the doctoral level, may also wish to work on their own methods development or other personal research projects, but they would not
have time to do so if 100% of their time was allocated to collaborative research projects. These issues can feel constraining to a collaborative
biostatistician who may be incentivized to leave for a different position that affords more flexibility and autonomy with time (Welty et al., 2013).
This funding model also creates challenges for the collaborative biostatistics unit as a whole. For example, if all of the collaborative biostatisti
cians in the unit are entirely funded on existing grants and contracts, nobody has available time to allocate to a new grant or contract when one is
ready to be initiated. One solution to this problem is to hire a new collaborative biostatistician; however, the time it takes to recruit, onboard, and
train a new employee is typically long and causes delays in starting work on the new grant or contract. Time for leadership and/or process
improvement for the collaborative biostatistics unit may also be limited under this funding model. It is not uncommon for collaborative biostatisti
cians in formal leadership positions within the unit to have reserved time for management and leadership (Hanlon et al., 2022), but the unit may
be hindered when others do not have reserved time to take on informal leadership and/or process improvement tasks as they arise. For example,
an entry-level collaborative biostatistician may envision a way to change the unit's shared workflow to improve reproducibility, but implementing
this change would take time that they do not have if 100% of their effort is allocated to specific research projects or groups.
These challenges could be mitigated or eliminated if a portion of each collaborative biostatistician's time was covered by what we call ‘flexible
funding’. We define ‘flexible funding’ as any funding that is not directly linked to a specific research project or allocated to work with a specific
research group. The purpose of this paper is to describe a proposed structure for flexible funding within a collaborative biostatistics unit and to
present results from a qualitative study focusing on the benefits and challenges associated with flexible funding from the perspective of collabora
tive biostatisticians who are currently working under the proposed flexible funding model.
1.2 | Proposed structure for flexible funding
The ideal amount of flexible funding for a collaborative biostatistician position varies, but the minimum required to mitigate the aforementioned
challenges is around 10%–20% of one's time. Lower amounts of flexible funding are typically not enough to avoid these challenges; for example,
5% of one's time amounts to only 2 h of a 40-h work week. Assuming that 1 h would usually be needed for a unit meeting or other required
administrative task, this would leave only 1 h for professional development, mentorship, leadership, additional administrative/service tasks or
any other activity that the collaborative biostatistician wishes to engage in to benefit themselves or their unit. Reserving at least 10% of a
collaborative biostatistician's time for these tasks is more reasonable. If the tasks allocated to this flexible funding include more time-consuming
endeavours such as methods development or other side projects, intensive professional development (e.g., a semester-long course), or mentorship
of early-career biostatisticians within the unit, reserving 20% of one's time via flexible funding would be advisable.
In some cases, even higher amounts of flexible funding are warranted. For example, when a new collaborative biostatistician is hired, it may
make sense to allocate 100% of their time to a flexible funding source while they are completing onboarding and training that is not directly linked
to a specific research project or group. For a new collaborative biostatistician hired in this manner, we refer to the 10%–20% minimum level of
flexible funding as ‘protected’ flexible funding, and the remaining 80%–90% initially allocated as ‘temporary’ flexible funding. Under this model,
the new collaborative biostatistician's temporary flexible funding will steadily decrease over time as it is replaced with funding from grants,
contracts, fee-for-service work, or other projects, but 10%–20% of their time, also known as 0.1–0.2 full-time equivalent (FTE), will always be
reserved on protected flexible funding.
This graduated flexible funding allocation strategy is beneficial to promote appropriately timed growth (i.e., hiring) for the collaborative biosta
tistics unit. For example, a collaborative biostatistics unit may wish to begin hiring for the next new position when the most recently hired
person's level of project-based funding grows to 0.5 FTE (i.e., 50% of their time). This strategy is illustrated in Figure 1. In Figure 1, the desired
base level of protected flexible funding for each person in the hypothetical collaborative biostatistics unit is 0.2 FTE. In Figure 1 Scenario A,
