Flexible funding may be drawn from multiple sources, but primarily two in this proposed model: a consistent amount of institutional ‘buy-in’
from the parent entity of the collaborative biostatistics unit (e.g., funds allocated from department-, centre-, or college-level budgets), or as a
portion of funds from contracts or memoranda of understanding (MOUs) with external collaborators' units for each fiscal year. While all funds
received from various sources may feed into a singular account for the entirety of the unit, consistent documentation is necessary to ensure
proper allocation of funds relative to the commitments to external contracts and agreements. By maintaining a singular account for the collabora
tive biostatistics unit—composed of static funds from the unit's parent entity (department, college, etc.) and funds from contracts or grants with
external collaborators—the ability of biostatisticians to have continual support, even during periods of fewer projects for any one biostatistician,
ensures the stability of the unit for the fiscal year. The nature of the agreement with the collaborating academic unit (e.g., contract, MOU)
allows for continuity on, minimally, an annual basis, if not longer. However, such agreements may be modified as part of a continual discussion
with leadership of collaborating units to determine the level of biostatistician support needed.
As it pertains to the proposed flexible funding model, the collaborative biostatistics unit studied in Sections 2 and 3—the Biostatistics Consult
ing and Interdisciplinary Research Collaboration Lab (Biostat CIRCL) at the University of Kentucky—is primarily funded by collaborative unit agree
ments, structured as MOUs, with numerous colleges, departments, divisions, and centres across the University of Kentucky campus. As part of
the MOU arrangement, counter to that of annual or one-time contracts, some portion of each collaborative unit's funds are allocated to CIRCL
biostatisticians' flexible funding. Additionally, a small, consistent allocation is provided by the Biostat CIRCL's parent entities to support general
administrative purchasing, technological infrastructure, travel funds and, as needed, flexible funding support.
