Sections 2 and 3 describe a qualitative study of collaborative biostatisticians who are currently working under the proposed flexible funding
model described above, including perspectives on the impact and benefits of the model. Section 4 addresses recommended sources of flexible
funding and additional challenges related to the implementation of the proposed model.
2 | METHODS
We conducted a qualitative study via one-on-one semi-structured interviews (Creswell, 2014) to examine collaborative biostatisticians'
experiences with the proposed flexible funding model. Eligible participants were collaborative biostatistician staff members in the Biostatistics
Consulting and Interdisciplinary Research Collaboration Lab (Biostat CIRCL) at the University of Kentucky. This study was determined to be
exempt from review by the University of Kentucky's Institutional Review Board (protocol #91815).
All Biostat CIRCL staff members (n = 6) were invited to participate in the study. Consent to participate and be recorded in a one-on-one
interview was obtained online via the University of Kentucky's Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) system (Harris et al., 2009). In-depth
interviews with each participant were conducted and recorded via Zoom in November 2023. Each one-on-one interview was facilitated by a
qualitative researcher and was between 10 and 35 min in duration. The semi-structured interviews were organized by an interview guide
containing open-ended questions on pre-determined focus areas related to flexible funding, including the use of time allocated to flexible funding,
team culture and dynamics, impact and benefits, and challenges and areas of improvement.
Recorded interviews were initially transcribed automatically using Zoom. Interview transcripts were then reviewed and verified for accuracy
by a member of the research team. Each interview was independently coded by two members of the research team using inductive analysis
(Bingham & Witkowsky, 2022), followed by a meeting between these research team members to discuss and resolve any discrepancies. Following
the completion of systematic coding, the coded interviews were synthesized to identify the primary themes using principles of thematic analysis
(Braun & Clarke, 2006). We report the primary themes and key quotes from participants within each of our focus areas (use of flexible funding
time, team culture and dynamics, impact and benefits, and challenges related to flexible funding).
3 | RESULTS
Six collaborative biostatistician staff members participated in the qualitative interviews. The participants were balanced by gender (three female,
three male) and training (two PhD, three Master of Science and one Master of Public Health). Most (5/6) were early career with less than 5 years
of experience as a collaborative biostatistician. The median time that participants had spent working in the Biostat CIRCL was approximately
2 years. Two participants worked remotely, and four worked in on-campus positions.
3.1 | Use of time allocated to flexible funding
Participants reported a wide range of different activities that they have engaged in as part of their time allocated to flexible funding, represented
by two main themes of ‘internal and operational’ activities and ‘external and engagement’ activities (Figure 2).
Internal and operational activities included three subcategories: (1) administrative tasks, (2) professional development and (3) unit initiatives
(Figure 2). Administrative tasks that participants mentioned included project management, such as tracking time and research outcomes from pro
jects, as well as project reflection, which refers to individual and/or team examinations of successes and areas of improvement in past or current
projects. It also included providing mentorship to junior-level collaborative biostatisticians or receiving mentorship from senior members of the
unit through activities such as meeting biweekly to discuss challenges on ongoing projects, how to effectively manage one's time across many
active projects, overall goals and career trajectory, and so forth. Mentorship was also mentioned in a more informal capacity, such as stopping into
a colleague's office to ask questions or chat about work-related issues as needed. Participants also mentioned technology management as an
administrative task that is accomplished through time allocated to flexible funding, which includes tasks such as adding code to GitHub or the
unit's R repository to improve efficiency for a future project. Finally, as part of administrative tasks, participants also mentioned attending regular
meetings for the collaborative biostatistics unit (1 h per week, required), the larger department (1 h per month, required) or the college (30 min
every other week, not required).
The second subcategory that emerged within the theme of internal and operational activities is professional development. Participants were
involved in a wide range of different professional development activities tailored to their individual needs and interests. These spanned activities
such as attending on-campus or virtual workshops and seminars, enrolling in formal courses, and self-study. A third subcategory of ‘unit initia
tives’ also emerged within the theme of internal and operational activities. Unit initiatives are closely related to professional development, distin
guished only by the group-based nature of unit initiatives as opposed to the individual nature of the aforementioned professional development
