3.3 | Impact and benefits of flexible funding
When asked to speak about the impact and benefits of flexible funding, participants' responses revealed themes related to professional develop
ment, work–life balance, and productivity. With respect to professional development, one early-career participant noted, ‘Kind of thinking
introspectively that I'm young, and so being able to build my CV like that while I'm still working ... has allowed me to grow in my position and be
confident in my field and what I'm working on.’ Participants also mentioned that having allocated time and encouragement to take advantage of
professional development opportunities was linked to job satisfaction and retention. As summarized by one participant, ‘We do work, but we're
not exclusively workers. We like to grow. And so with that, the flex time allows us to grow, not just as workers, but also as professionals. Which is
an incredible, unique aspect that we're able to grow rather than just being in a stagnant position. Which I think helps with that issue that we see
with retention of people who just kind of get bored in these jobs.’
Several participants also discussed how flexible funding was linked to their work–life balance and general productivity. One mentioned
that without flexible funding ‘... I'd be working 7 days a week’. Another mentioned that ‘[flexible funding] kind of just like rejuvenates me and
makes me happy to come to work. And then, ... the happier you are at work generally translates to the other aspects of your life’. Concerning
productivity during the workday, multiple participants mentioned that having time to decompress when transitioning between projects (referred
to as ‘project reflection’ in Figure 2) or being able to break up a long day of coding with an activity such as attending a seminar has made them
more efficient overall in their project-related work.
3.4 | Challenges related to flexible funding
When asked about existing challenges or improvements that could be made, participants indicated that encountering problems related to how
they engage in flexible funding activities was rare. One participant commented on the occasional inconsistency in the available time in a given
week relative to specific project-related tasks like grant proposals or abstract submissions; another cited difficulty in deciding the optimal way to
prioritize their flexible funding activities.
Overall, participants shared that potential challenges were typically avoided by (1) having autonomy in decisions about what to do with their
time allocated to flexible funding and (2) having an established culture within the unit around the value of professional development. When
thinking about challenges that may exist for other units to implement the proposed flexible funding model, one participant explained that it might
be challenging to ‘convince folks that it [flexible funding] is a valuable thing. I would imagine at a lot of institutions that would be a very hard sell.
At ours, I think the value is evident, and I think that people are seeing that’.
4 | DISCUSSION
This paper details a proposed model for flexible funding within a collaborative biostatistics unit and highlights the benefits of implementing such a
model. Activities undertaken during time allocated to a flexible funding source can create an enjoyable, dynamic, and cooperative work environ
ment within a collaborative biostatistics unit. In addition to creating a sense of community through mentorship, team-oriented problem solving,
and working together on unit initiatives, flexible funding also provides time for collaborative biostatisticians to pursue opportunities that interest
them and promote their desired career trajectory, which can lead to increased job satisfaction and thus retention within the unit.
The perspectives represented in the qualitative study are from staff collaborative biostatisticians who are not responsible for unit administra
tion or leadership. As such, challenges related to implementing the proposed flexible funding model from an administrative and financial
standpoint are somewhat underappreciated in the qualitative study results. As detailed in Figure 1, the amount of flexible funding required to
implement the proposed model within a collaborative biostatistics unit grows with the number of biostatisticians in the unit; for this reason, the
