total amount of flexible funding needed for the unit could be quite large. Because activities performed as part of flexible funding are not linked to
specific collaborative research projects, we recommend that institutional-level funds be used to support flexible funding time. If the collaborative
biostatistics unit has not previously been supported by any institutional-level funding, it can be challenging to advocate for it. For leaders of col
laborative biostatistics units who are seeking institutional-level flexible funding, it may be especially beneficial to focus their advocacy on the
impact of flexible funding on the retention of collaborative biostatistics staff, sustainability of the hiring cycle (as demonstrated in Figure 1),
improved productivity on projects, and increased national recognition (e.g., through activities such as networking in professional organizations).
An additional outcome of employing a flexible funding model to support a collaborative biostatistics unit is the ability to hire and continually
support administrative staff, such as a research project manager or unit administrative assistant. Under this funding structure, such a position
would be financially structured similarly to those of the biostatisticians, could be maintained irrespective of any single grant or contract, and would
not be split between several different exclusive funding sources, but rather supported by the unit.
We also encourage unit leadership to develop a mechanism for documenting the activities undertaken as part of time allocated to flexible
funding. While some activities are expected to be mundane (such as time spent attending regular administrative meetings), oftentimes, the activities
undertaken as part of flexible funding time do result in impressive outcomes that can be used to advocate for a continued source of flexible funds for
the unit (activities such as improving reproducibility in the unit's workflow or developing and presenting a new method at a national conference). If it
is not possible to obtain flexible funding at the institutional level, we recommend that leaders of collaborative biostatistics units consider whether flex
ible time could be incorporated into existing project-based contracts or establish retainer contracts or MOUs with collaborating academic units. While
oftentimes difficult for grant-funded projects, it may be possible to incorporate flexible time into long-term contracts that provide funding for the col
laborative biostatistics unit from a research group that is internal to the institution (such as an individual department), as described by Desai et al.
(2022). For collaborative biostatistics units funded largely by these internal contracts, the benefits of flexible time may be achieved through this route.
While the fiscal efficiency of internal, centralized biostatistics units is not novel (Parker, 2000), nor are the limitations of traditional
fee-for-service or percent-effort models (Perkins et al., 2016), flexible funding models are far less widely used and understood, limiting the degree
to which we can compare this model to others. However, Parker (2000) describes the value of a centralized biostatistics consulting unit supported
by several long-term grants to provide approximately $6 of value added per dollar spent. Further, Perkins et al. (2016) describe a funding model
structured around collaborating academic units or broad institutional support to carry many of the same benefits to biostatisticians and external
collaborators as other long-term percent-effort arrangements. We expect that these benefits—including the financial efficiencies of internal,
centralized biostatistics units—are entirely applicable in the proposed flexible funding model.
In addition to providing information for leaders of collaborative biostatistics units, the results of this study may also be useful to those
searching for jobs within collaborative biostatistics units at universities or academic medical centres. Funding models have a major impact on
day-to-day expectations and autonomy within an academic collaborative biostatistics position, and this autonomy (or lack thereof) may greatly
impact one's job satisfaction and ability to progress in their career. We recommend that those interviewing for an academic collaborative
biostatistics position inquire about the funding model of their potential position and whether it offers dedicated time for administrative tasks,
professional development, networking, and so forth.
We acknowledge that there is not a one-size-fits-all funding model for collaborative biostatistics units in universities and academic medical
centres. Collaborative biostatistics units vary in size, structure, workload, mission, and available funding sources, all of which could impact the
implementation of a flexible funding model. However, the proposed flexible funding approach has the capacity to be tailored to an individual unit's
needs and constraints while still providing the many benefits detailed in this paper. In our experience, the success of implementing a flexible
funding model depends primarily on two factors: (1) gaining support, fiscal and otherwise, from institutional leadership and (2) developing a
culture within the unit that empowers collaborative biostatisticians to take advantage of flexible funding time through activities that support their
professional growth or improve the operations of the unit. Ultimately, the increase in demand for biostatistics consulting and collaboration at
universities and academic medical centres further supports the discussion of continuous funding models for collaborative biostatisticians.
Evaluation and reassessment of a collaborative biostatistics unit's current funding structure(s) could identify strategic focus areas to promote
sustainable funding models while accounting for the unit's current and future organization, resources, and efforts.
